    ☒ Re-read this checklist and the roadmap entry in `docs/project.todo` before editing runtime code.
    ☒ Inventory the active checkout, branch, worktree state, index state, installed rock version, and current package archive contents.
    ☒ Confirm the current runtime and declaration signatures for `ds.root`, `ds.get`, `ds.inspect`, `ds.capture_screen`, and every `Subject` query method.
    ☒ Confirm the current mount categories and how each category exposes its root, subject adapter, interaction target, window size, and render tracker.
    ☒ Confirm zero-based inclusive bounds for Lua views, native widgets, pointer commands, and screen captures.
    ☒ Confirm the locally installed DFHack contract for `dfhack.screen.getWindowSize()` and `dfhack.screen.readTile(x, y)`.
    ☒ Reproduce and record `readTile()` behavior for ordinary text cells, blank cells, graphical cells, invalid cells, and TrueType mode where available.
    ☒ Record the pre-change focused unit-test baseline for subject, mount-command, adapter, and diagnostic coverage.
    ☒ Record the pre-change full standalone unit-suite result.
    ☒ Record the pre-change Lua 5.3 and Lua 5.4 syntax and formatting results.
    ☒ Record focused pre-change live results separately for ordinary widget, overlay widget, complete screen, and borrowed native screen mounts.
    ☒ Stop and update this checklist if current DFHack cannot expose rendered character bytes consistently enough to support the locked contract.
        - No stop was required. DFHack exposed all 19,800 cells in the active
          graphical screen as readable byte-bearing pens. TrueType was not
          active in the available environment, and its documented nil-cell
          behavior remains an explicit limitation and later live gate.

Phase 0 evidence:
    - Checkout: `world-mount-system` at
      `39e137ead8a2b3d57c93bbba89a166ab7ce17b11`, ahead of
      `origin/world-mount-system` by two commits, with an empty index and
      worktree before the evidence update.
    - Source package: `dwarfspec-0.2.1-1.rockspec`. User-level LuaRocks still
      had `dwarfspec 0.2.0-1` installed for Lua 5.4. The initial live command
      correctly rejected the already-loaded 0.2.0 service; DFHack was
      restarted before any live baseline was accepted.
    - Current archive: `dist/dwarfspec-0.2.1-1.all.rock`, 220,008 bytes,
      modified 2026-07-31 01:10:52 -07:00. Its 99 entries were 92 Lua files,
      four documents, one command, the rockspec, and `rock_manifest`; it
      included `lua/ds/d.lua` and contained zero tests and zero native
      binaries.
    - Runtime and declarations agreed on:
        - `ds.root(options)` / `DS.root(options)`;
        - `ds.get(control_path, options)` /
          `DS.get(control_path, options)`;
        - `ds.inspect(view)` / `DS.inspect(view)`; and
        - `ds.capture_screen(name, options)` /
          `DS.capture_screen(name, options)`.
    - Runtime and declarations both exposed the same 11 Subject methods:
      `click`, `hover`, `move_pointer`, `mouseWheel`, `input`, `type`,
      `redraw`, `inspect`, `getFocusList`, `text`, and `raw`.
    - Mount categories were `widget`, `overlay`, `screen`, and borrowed
      `native`. Owned mounts expose their component root, owned host screen,
      Lua-view subject adapter, owned interaction target, and render tracker
      instrumented around `onRender`. Native mounts expose a pinned viewscreen,
      native-widget subject adapter, borrowed interaction target, render
      tracker, and reversible overlay-dispatch render observer. All use the
      current window-size service supplied to `ds`.
    - Lua-view bounds are the live `frame_body`; native-widget bounds are
      normalized zero-based inclusive rectangles and offer window-clipped
      interaction bounds. Pointer documentation uses zero-based UI-grid
      coordinates. `capture_screen()` reads `x=0..width-1` and
      `y=0..height-1` into one-based Lua row storage.
    - Installed DFHack was `53.15-r2 (release)`. Its installed Lua API states
      that `getWindowSize()` returns width and height and `readTile()` returns
      a pen or nil for invalid or TrueType cells.
    - Live read-only probe in graphics mode at 264 by 75 cells:
      `readable=19800`, `unreadable=0`, `printable=197`, `blank=19611`,
      `graphical=1485`, and both negative and beyond-window reads returned
      nil. Ordinary, blank, and graphical readable-cell behavior was observed.
      TrueType behavior was unavailable because the active screen exposed no
      nil cells; no display or configuration change was made to force it.
    - Focused standalone baseline:
      `subject_spec.lua`, `ds_mount_commands_spec.lua`,
      `lua_view_adapter_spec.lua`, `native_widget_adapter_spec.lua`,
      `mount_adapters_spec.lua`, and `automation_diagnostics_spec.lua`
      completed with 118 successes, zero failures, zero errors, and zero
      pending.
    - Full standalone baseline: 592 successes, zero failures, zero errors,
      and zero pending under Lua 5.4.6.
    - Syntax and formatting baseline: `Tools/Check-Lua.ps1` passed 271 files
      with Lua 5.4.6. The embedded DFHack Lua 5.3 runtime parsed the same 271
      repository files plus the temporary probe itself; formatting checks are
      byte-oriented and were supplied by the 5.4 tool run. The temporary probe
      and its result file were removed after evidence capture.
    - Focused live baseline after the service restart:
        - ordinary widget: 7 successes, cleanup confirmed;
        - mounted overlay: 5 successes, cleanup confirmed;
        - complete screen: 2 successes, cleanup confirmed; and
        - borrowed native screen: 2 successes, cleanup confirmed.
      Post-run service status reported version 0.2.1, executor idle, queue
      zero, and quarantine none.
    - Probe live run: 2 successes with `cleanup_confirmed=true`. An earlier
      probe attempt enumerated the Dwarf Fortress directory instead of the
      project and failed on a non-project Lua 5.4 file; cleanup was still
      confirmed, the root was corrected, and the successful rerun supplied
      the recorded evidence.

Phase 1 - Add canonical authoring types and API declarations:
    ☒ Add the canonical `dwarfspec.ScreenRect` declaration without duplicating or drifting from existing inspected-rectangle types.
    ☒ Add the canonical `dwarfspec.TextSearchQuery` declaration.
    ☒ Add the canonical `dwarfspec.TextSearchArea` declaration.
    ☒ Add the `DS.search(query, search_area?)` declaration.
    ☒ Add the `Subject:search(query)` declaration.
    ☒ Document literal case-sensitive matching, row-major occurrence ordering, nil-on-miss behavior, coordinate conventions, and unreadable-region failure behavior at the declarations.
    ☒ Document that subject scoping is spatial and cannot prove render ownership.
    ☒ Add language-appropriate documentation comments for every new public and internal class and method.
    ☒ Extend declaration fixtures with valid root, subject, and rectangle searches.
    ☒ Add negative declaration fixtures for missing text, invalid text, invalid occurrence, malformed rectangles, and unsupported search-area types.
    ☒ Verify that an installed-rock consumer discovers all search types without adding the source checkout to the LuaLS library path.

Phase 1 evidence:
    - `dwarfspec.ScreenRect` is the required-coordinate canonical rectangle.
      `dwarfspec.SubjectInspectRect` now inherits it and declares only its
      optional clipped-coordinate extension, avoiding a second copy of the
      base coordinate fields.
    - `dwarfspec.TextSearchQuery` and `dwarfspec.ScreenRect` are exact LuaLS
      classes. The query requires `text`, allows integer `occurrence`, and
      rejects injected unknown fields. Runtime-only value constraints remain
      assigned to the validation work below.
    - `DS.search(query, search_area?)` and `Subject:search(query)` declare
      `dwarfspec.ScreenRect|nil` results and document exact matching, row-major
      occurrence selection, zero-based inclusive coordinates, readable
      misses, wholly unreadable regions, and spatial-only subject scoping.
    - The valid declaration fixture covers root-default, subject, and explicit
      rectangle searches and references every new public type. LuaLS 3.18.2
      checked it with zero problems against the source declaration.
    - The invalid declaration fixture produced exactly six intended LuaLS
      warnings: missing text, non-string text, non-integer occurrence, unknown
      query field, missing rectangle coordinate, and unsupported boolean area.
    - `luarocks make` installed the current source package into the temporary
      `.package-declaration-test` tree with dependency resolution disabled.
      That tree contained `share/lua/5.4/ds/d.lua`; LuaLS checked the valid
      consumer with zero problems while its only project library entry was the
      installed tree. The source checkout was absent from that configuration,
      and the temporary tree was removed after verification.
    - Full standalone unit suite: 592 successes, zero failures, zero errors,
      and zero pending.
    - Lua 5.4.6 syntax and formatting passed all 273 Lua files. Runtime,
      focused live, package-archive, and Lua 5.3 gates were not rerun because
      this work changed declarations, authoring fixtures, and planning
      evidence only.

Phase 2 - Implement pure query and rectangle normalization:
    ☒ Add `src/dwarfspec/commands/text_search.lua` as the focused dependency-injected command module for text-search validation and matching.
    ☒ Add `tests/unit/text_search_command_spec.lua` as its focused standalone unit-test file.
    ☒ Validate that the query is a table before reading fields.
    ☒ Reject unknown query fields with a stable error that names the field.
    ☒ Validate `text` as a nonempty string.
    ☒ Reject NUL, carriage-return, and newline bytes explicitly.
    ☒ Validate `occurrence` as a positive integer and apply the default of `1`.
    ☒ Copy normalized query values so caller mutation cannot alter an in-progress search.
    ☒ Validate all rectangle coordinates as integers.
    ☒ Reject inverted rectangles.
    ☒ Implement inclusive rectangle intersection.
    ☒ Return a distinct empty-intersection result so invalid input, an empty effective area, and an unreadable area cannot be conflated.
    ☒ Add focused unit tests for every validation branch, boundary coordinate, clipping case, and caller-mutation case.

Phase 2 evidence:
    - `dwarfspec.commands.text_search.normalize_query()` validates the table
      shape,
      closed field set, nonempty single-row byte string, and positive integral
      occurrence, then returns a copied query with occurrence defaulted to one.
    - `normalize_rectangle()` validates and copies all four integral inclusive
      coordinates and reports horizontal and vertical inversion separately.
      Negative coordinates remain valid input for later window clipping.
    - `intersect_rectangles()` performs inclusive intersection on validated
      copies. Disjoint rectangles return the identity-stable
      `EMPTY_INTERSECTION` sentinel, while invalid inputs fail validation;
      later rendered-cell work retains a separate unreadable-region failure.
    - Focused standalone unit result: 16 successes, zero failures, zero
      errors, and zero pending. Coverage includes query type, unknown fields,
      text type and emptiness, each forbidden byte, occurrence default and
      failures, coordinate failures, both inversion axes, negative and zero
      boundaries, four-edge clipping, inclusive edge and point contact,
      horizontal and vertical disjointness, operand validation, fresh results,
      and caller-mutation isolation.
    - Full standalone unit result: 608 successes, zero failures, zero errors,
      and zero pending.
    - Lua 5.4.6 syntax and formatting passed all 275 Lua files. DFHack's
      embedded Lua 5.3 parser separately accepted the new module and focused
      unit file. The temporary parser probe was removed after verification.
    - Rendered-cell reading and matching remain deliberately unimplemented;
      those behaviors and their tests belong to the next checklist section.

Phase 3 - Implement deterministic rendered-cell matching:
    ☒ Inject window-size and tile-reading functions into the command module so its algorithm is unit-testable without DFHack globals.
    ☒ Capture the current window dimensions once per search.
    ☒ Intersect the requested scope with the captured window rectangle before reading cells.
    ☒ Read only the effective rectangle.
    ☒ Convert readable `pen.ch` values into exact one-byte row strings without consulting colors, tiles, or adapted component text.
    ☒ Treat nil pens, missing `ch`, nonintegral `ch`, and out-of-byte-range `ch` as unreadable match-breaking sentinels.
    ☒ Preserve readable NUL-valued blank cells as readable nonmatching bytes.
    ☒ Search each row with plain literal matching.
    ☒ Count matches in deterministic top-to-bottom, then left-to-right order.
    ☒ Count overlapping occurrences by advancing one cell after each match start.
    ☒ Stop after the requested occurrence is found.
    ☒ Return a fresh plain rectangle with exact text-cell bounds.
    ☒ Return `nil` when at least one cell was readable but the requested occurrence was absent.
    ☒ Fail with a bounded error when no cell in the effective region was readable.
    ☒ Avoid allocating pen copies or a full `ds.capture_screen()` evidence record.
    ☒ Add unit tests for left and right edges, single-cell text, repeated text, overlapping text, occurrence selection, clipped matches, unreadable gaps, fully unreadable regions, and no-match readable regions.
    ☒ Add a bounded performance characterization for the default 128 by 64 component viewport without establishing an unstable wall-clock gate.

Phase 3 evidence:
    - `dwarfspec.commands.text_search.new()` requires injected
      `get_window_size` and `read_tile` functions and returns the isolated
      matcher. It captures and validates window geometry once, intersects the
      requested scope with that window, and returns `EMPTY_INTERSECTION`
      without tile reads when they do not overlap.
    - Each effective row is represented by exact one-byte `pen.ch` values.
      Nil pens, inaccessible or missing `ch`, fractional values, and values
      outside 0 through 255 become NUL match-breaking sentinels. A genuine
      readable NUL remains counted as readable, and byte 255 matches exactly.
      Pen colors, tiles, and adapted text are never read or copied.
    - Plain row searches advance one cell after each match start, preserving
      overlaps. Occurrences are selected top-to-bottom then left-to-right.
      The matcher returns a fresh zero-based inclusive rectangle immediately
      after the selected row is searched and does not read subsequent rows.
    - A readable miss or unavailable requested occurrence returns nil. A
      wholly unreadable effective region fails with the bounded rectangle
      diagnostic `text search effective region has no readable screen cells`.
    - Focused standalone unit result: 36 successes, zero failures, zero
      errors, and zero pending. The cases cover dependency and window
      validation, clipping and read bounds, empty intersections, horizontal
      edges, single cells, repeated, overlapping, and row-major occurrences,
      selected-occurrence early exit, clipped matches, unreadable gaps and
      character values, readable NUL, byte 255, style independence, fresh
      results, in-progress caller mutation, readable misses, and wholly
      unreadable regions.
    - The bounded 128 by 64 characterization performed one window-size call
      and exactly 8,192 tile reads for a full readable miss. It establishes no
      wall-clock threshold.
    - Full standalone unit result: 628 successes, zero failures, zero errors,
      and zero pending. Lua 5.4.6 syntax and formatting passed all 275 Lua
      files, and DFHack's embedded Lua 5.3 parser accepted the command module
      and focused unit file. The temporary parser probe was removed.

Phase 4 - Resolve mount and search-area scopes:
    ☒ Add a small `ds.search()` command body that delegates validation, scope resolution, and matching to the text-search command module.
    ☒ Require the current mount through the existing mount-context lifecycle guard.
    ☒ Resolve a borrowed native default to the full captured DF window.
    ☒ Resolve an owned mount default from the root adapter's visible body bounds.
    ☒ Resolve an explicit subject through the existing current-mount subject descriptor and adapter.
    ☒ Require effective subject visibility where the adapter exposes it.
    ☒ Use current clipped interaction bounds when they are available; otherwise normalize and clip the adapter's body bounds.
    ☒ Resolve an explicit rectangle without interpreting it as a component option or subject descriptor.
    ☒ Intersect every explicit area with both the mount default scope and current window.
    ☒ Return `nil` without reading cells when a valid explicit rectangle has an empty effective intersection.
    ☒ Preserve existing stale-subject, replaced-native-root, cleaned-mount, foreign-mount, and inactive-screen errors.
    ☒ Ensure source-selected native and registered-overlay subjects retain their existing adapter and ownership validation.
    ☒ Ensure searching does not change `mount.command_subject`, render generation, pointer state, cleanup state, or retained capture state.
    ☒ Add focused mount-command tests for every mount category and search-area form.
    ☒ Add focused failures for missing mount, stale subject, foreign subject, hidden subject, missing bounds, inverted rectangle, and unreadable effective region.

Phase 4 evidence:
    - `ds.search()` is a thin public entry point that delegates to the
      constructed text-search command. The command module requires the current
      mount and active interaction target, copies and validates the query, and
      owns default-scope resolution, explicit-area resolution, rectangle
      intersection, and rendered matching.
    - Borrowed native mounts pass an omitted scope so the matcher uses its one
      captured full-window rectangle. Widget, overlay, and complete-screen
      mounts use the visible root body. Explicit rectangles are intersected
      with that mount default and then with the matcher's captured window.
      Empty explicit intersections return nil before any tile read while still
      validating the query.
    - Explicit subjects re-enter `resolve_subject()` and retain its mount,
      source, adapter, identity, and native-screen validation. Search requires
      inherited native visibility where available, otherwise direct adapter
      visibility. It prefers current clipped interaction bounds and falls back
      to normalized body bounds for Lua views and adapters without that API.
    - Focused coverage proves native and registered-overlay sources, clipped
      native bounds, inherited native invisibility, Lua child bounds, all four
      mount categories, default and explicit scopes, and exact returned
      rectangles. It also preserves stale or foreign mounts, replaced native
      widgets, missing mount, inactive screen, hidden subject, missing or
      offscreen bounds, inverted or unsupported rectangles, and wholly
      unreadable-region errors.
    - The read-only state test observed unchanged render generation, pointer
      coordinates and ownership, cleanup count, capture table and contents,
      invalidation count, and command-event count. A following root redraw was
      still attributed to `<root>`, proving search did not leave
      `mount.command_subject` set.
    - Focused public mount-command result: 93 successes, zero failures, zero
      errors, and zero pending. Focused command-module result: 37 successes,
      zero failures, zero errors, and zero pending.
    - Full standalone unit result: 637 successes, zero failures, zero errors,
      and zero pending. Lua 5.4.6 syntax and formatting passed all 275 Lua
      files. DFHack's embedded Lua 5.3 parser accepted both changed production
      files and both focused unit files; the temporary probe was removed.
    - Live component, overlay, complete-screen, and borrowed-native proofs
      remain assigned to their later checklist work.

Phase 5 - Add fluent subject search and public inventory coverage:
    ☒ Implement `Subject:search(query)` through the subject command-dispatch path.
    ☒ Preserve the subject object and descriptor without retaining search results.
    ☒ Register the search handler in `context.mount_context.subject_commands`.
    ☒ Add `search` to the public subject inventory in `src/dwarfspec/component.lua`.
    ☒ Reconcile every command applicability or conformance inventory that enumerates DS and Subject methods.
    ☒ Add focused `subject_spec.lua` coverage for delegation, result passthrough, nil passthrough, lifecycle failure, and argument validation ownership.
    ☒ Add focused conformance coverage proving `ds.search(query, subject)` and `subject:search(query)` use the same effective area and result.

Phase 5 evidence:
    - `Subject:search(query)` delegates through `invoke_subject_command()` to
      the `search` entry in `mount_context.subject_commands`, which calls
      `ds.search(query, subject)`. It returns the result directly and retains
      neither result nor altered subject descriptor state.
    - The public subject inventory, component-boundary regression, and custom
      command-reservation schema include `search`; `src/ds.d.lua` exposes the
      matching `Subject:search(query)` declaration.
    - Focused subject coverage proves result and nil passthrough, descriptor
      preservation, run-context lifecycle rejection, and dispatch ownership.
      Mount-command coverage proves direct and fluent subject forms use the
      same bounds and that malformed fluent queries retain subject-failure
      context with the underlying search validation cause.
    - Full standalone unit suite: 637 successes, zero failures, zero errors,
      and zero pending. Lua 5.4.6 syntax and formatting passed 275 Lua files;
      `git diff --check` passed.

